        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
It is hypothesized that changes in the baroreceptor reflex play a major role in the development of conditions responsible for the fall of blood pressure (orthostatic hypotension) after space flight. This is especially pronounced in many astronauts when they try to stand up shortly after their return to Earth; they may feel faint and some are unable to remain standing. Although this and other effects associated with cardiovascular deconditioning disappear within a relatively short time period (days), all efforts must be made to understand the causes of this condition which affects the health, safety, and productivity of the astronauts. 
<P>
Changes in arterial pressure are detected by pressure sensors called baroreceptors, located in two areas of the arterial tree: the bifurcation of the carotid arteries in the neck and the arch of the aorta, located in the chest. Since carotid baroreceptors are more accessible, this protocol focuses on them. 
<P>
Previous experiments showed that changes of the intra-carotid pressure can cause changes of the heart period. The relationship was identified as a sigmoidal curve, consisting of a threshold region, a linear portion, and a saturation range. In the threshold range, pressure within the carotid sinus must rise to a certain level before heart period prolongation occurs. In the linear range, heart period increases more or less linearly as intra-carotid pressure rises. In the saturation range, additional increments of intra-carotid pressure do not increase heart period. With the intervals between each heart beat rising, the diastolic blood pressure decreases. 
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
The investigation used the technique of neck suction. A neck chamber made of silicone rubber, which is molded to fit the contour of the anterior three fourths of the neck, is the centerpiece of the system. It has a silastic rubber diaphragm inside the chamber which seals against the neck during positive pressure and a gel edge which seals when suction is applied. The system was programmed to deliver a complex baroreceptor stimulus for use in space. First, the pressure inside the diaphragm was increased to about 40 mmHg. The positive pressure reduced carotid sinus diameter, and in most subjects, reduced the level of afferent (toward the brain) carotid baroreceptor activity to levels below the threshold necessary to provoke cardiographic changes. After about 5 seconds a series of ECG triggered pressure reductions was delivered which lowered the neck chamber pressure in a stepwise fashion to 25, 10, -5, -20, -35, -50, and -65 mmHg. The external application of negative pressure stretched the wall of the carotid artery and the carotid sinuses were stimulated and responded. After the last pressure reduction, neck chamber pressure returned to ambient levels. For each subject the stimulus train was applied seven times and the results were averaged. 
<P>
Once the ECG electrodes and the neck chamber were properly attached, the pressure reduction sequence was initiated. Each subject was required to complete two normal respiratory cycles, holding his breath (approximately 15 seconds) after the second expiration. The complex baroreceptor stimulus then was applied. In addition, plasma catecholamine levels were measured from blood samples obtained during other D-2 experiments. 
<P>
Experiment sessions were completed preflight, inflight and postflight. During data collection on Earth, all subjects assumed a recumbent positon (the experiment operations were done by other personnel). During the flight the astronauts performed the procedures by themselves. For each astronaut, four inflight sessions were planned. 
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
On landing day, baseline R-R intervals and their standard deviations were significantly below pre-launch values.  Both maximum slopes and R-R interval ranges were significantly reduced 2 and 10 days  post flight.  The data document transient reductions of baseline vagal-cardiac neural outflow, and protracted impairment of vagal responses to baroreceptor stimulation after space missions.
<P>
        </div>

